1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,000
This is Spotter 2: a trip planner that turns three locations and a cycle-hours
figure into a legal route and a full set of driver's daily logs.

2
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:17,000
Everything starts in the trip form - and the location fields are live. Typing
three characters queries the backend's place search, so you pick a real
geocoded place.

3
00:00:17,000 --> 00:00:24,000
Three inputs - where the truck is now, the pickup and the drop-off - plus the
hours already burned in the 70-hour cycle.

4
00:00:24,000 --> 00:00:31,000
An optional panel pins the departure time and fills the log header: carrier,
tractor, trailer, shipper, commodity and load ID.

5
00:00:31,000 --> 00:00:35,000
One click posts the trip to the API.

6
00:00:35,000 --> 00:00:45,000
A few seconds later the whole run comes back - miles, arrival time and a
legality summary.

7
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:56,000
The road route itself comes from OSRM, drawn over OpenStreetMap tiles.

8
00:00:56,000 --> 00:01:01,000
Every required stop is pinned on it - rests, fuel, pickup and drop-off - at
the exact mile it has to happen.

9
00:01:01,000 --> 00:01:19,000
The itinerary is the human-readable version of that same plan: each stop with
its time, place and the reason for it.

10
00:01:19,000 --> 00:01:27,000
Summary tiles keep the numbers honest, including how much of the 70-hour cycle
has been eaten.

11
00:01:27,000 --> 00:01:37,000
But the real deliverable is the log book - a fully drawn FMCSA daily log sheet
for every calendar day of the trip.

12
00:01:37,000 --> 00:01:48,000
A 24-hour grid, duty-status lines drawn to the quarter hour, brackets,
numbered remark flags, per-line totals and the seven-day recap.

13
00:01:48,000 --> 00:01:56,000
One sheet per day. Day two carries the tail of the run and closes out the
cycle.

14
00:01:56,000 --> 00:02:06,000
So how does it work? The backend is Django with DRF, and the heart of it is
the hours-of-service engine.

15
00:02:06,000 --> 00:02:17,000
The limits live in one place - eleven driving hours, fourteen on-duty hours, a
thirty-minute break and the seventy-hour cycle - and every driving chunk is
capped by the tightest of them.

16
00:02:17,000 --> 00:02:29,000
The simulator walks the route leg by leg, and before each chunk it asks what
the law requires next: a break, a reset, fuel, or a 34-hour restart.

17
00:02:29,000 --> 00:02:40,000
One subtlety the tests forced out - on-duty blocks are indivisible, so they
need their own cycle look-ahead or a block could slip past seventy hours.

18
00:02:40,000 --> 00:02:50,000
A second pass turns those segments into 24-hour grids, clipping each day at
midnight.

19
00:02:50,000 --> 00:02:59,000
Remarks follow the regulation: one numbered flag per duty-status change, and
none for a carry-over block that only squares the grid.

20
00:02:59,000 --> 00:03:09,000
The planner wires geocoding, routing and the log builder together into a
single response.

21
00:03:09,000 --> 00:03:18,000
Routing calls OSRM and interpolates position along the returned polyline, so
any mile marker maps to a real coordinate.

22
00:03:18,000 --> 00:03:29,000
And every one of these rules is pinned by a test - the eleven hours, the
fourteen, the break, the reset and the fuel interval.

23
00:03:29,000 --> 00:03:41,000
Forty-three tests covering the engine, the log sheets and the API surface -
all offline, no network.

24
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:51,000
And the end-to-end contract check - six hundred and thirty-three assertions
against a real six-hundred-mile trip - passes too.

25
00:03:51,000 --> 00:04:00,000
Spotter 2: one POST, a legal route, and finished log sheets you could hand to
an inspector.

